          Fragment:md
            ## Installation

            To use nmbl in Vue projects, you need the Vite plugin (for compilation) and the Vue language plugin (for IDE intellisense):

          .md-indent
            InstallWidget(packages="@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin @nmbl-lang/vue-language-plugin" dev={true})
          Fragment:md
            ## Configuration

            ### Vite Config

            Add the nmbl plugin to your `vite.config.ts`. It must be listed **before** the Vue plugin:

            ```ts title="vite.config.ts"
            import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
            import vue from '@vitejs/plugin-vue';
            import nmbl from '@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin';

            export default defineConfig({
              plugins: [
                nmbl(),  // must be before vue()
                vue(),
              ],
            });
            ```

            ### TypeScript Config

            Add the nmbl plugin to your `tsconfig.json` to enable intellisense:

            ```json title="tsconfig.json"
            {
              "compilerOptions": {
                // ... your existing options
              },
              "vueCompilerOptions": {
                "plugins": ["@nmbl-lang/vue-language-plugin"],
                "strictTemplates": true
              }
            }
            ```

            `strictTemplates` is optional but recommended: it makes Vue flag unknown components and **unknown props** (not just wrong-typed ones) in your nmbl templates, exactly as it would in a plain `<template>`.

            ### VS Code Setup

            The nmbl VS Code extension provides syntax highlighting — [install it from the VS Code Marketplace](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=nmbl.nmbl). Vue intellisense works through the Vue - Official extension (Volar).

            ## Basic Usage

            Add `lang="nmbl"` to your Vue template block, then write the markup as nmbl:

            ```vue title="App.vue"
            <script setup lang="ts">...</script>

            <template lang="nmbl">
            div#app
              header.site-header
                h1 {{ title }}
                nav
                  a(href="/home") Home
                  a(href="/about") About

              main.content
                article(v-for="post in posts" :key="post.id")
                  h2 {{ post.title }}
                  p {{ post.excerpt }}
                  button(@click="viewPost(post.id)") Read More

              // embedded markdown!
              .prose:md
                ## some title

                - list item1 with [link](https)
                - **bold** list item

              footer
                p © 2024 My Site
            </template>

            <style scoped>...</style>
            ```

            ## Control Flow

            nmbl has `@if` / `@elseif` / `@else` and `@each` blocks that compile to Vue's native `v-if` and `v-for`:

            ```nmbl framework=vue
            @if(user)
              p Welcome back, {{ user.name }}
            @elseif(guest)
              p Hello, guest
            @else
              a(href="/login") Log in

            ul
              @each(post of posts :key="post.id")
                li {{ post.title }}
            ```

            `@if` becomes `<template v-if="…">` and `@each` becomes `<template v-for="… of …" :key="…">`. The conditions and loop expressions are passed straight through, so you write them exactly as you would in a Vue template. Plain `v-if` / `v-for` directives still work too — the `@`-blocks are just optional sugar.

            ## Comments

            `//` comments are stripped from the output (use them for dev notes); `//!` comments are rendered as real HTML comments:

            ```nmbl framework=vue
            // dev note — never shipped
            //! becomes <!-- an HTML comment -->
            p Visible text
            ```

            You can also comment out individual props inside a tag's parens — handy while debugging:

            ```nmbl framework=vue
            button(
              type="submit"
              // disabled
              /* aria-label="Save" */
            ) Save
            ```

            ## Intellisense Features

            With the Vue language plugin installed, you get full intellisense support:

          .features-grid
            .feature
              h4 🔍 Go to Definition
              p Click on variables, methods, or components to jump to their definitions
            .feature
              h4 💡 Auto-completion
              p Get suggestions for props, events, directives, and component names
            .feature
              h4 🔗 CSS Class Linking
              p Ctrl/Cmd+click on classes to jump to their CSS definitions (scoped styles only)
            .feature
              h4 ❗ Error Highlighting
              p See TypeScript and Vue compiler errors directly in your template
            .feature
              h4 📝 Hover Information
              p Hover over variables and components to see type information
            .feature
              h4 ♻️ Refactoring
              p Rename variables and have them update across template and script

          Fragment:md
            ## Troubleshooting

          .troubleshooting:md
            ### Variables marked as unused

            Make sure the Vue language plugin is properly configured in your `tsconfig.json` and restart your IDE.

            ### No component / tag completion

            Component name, HTML tag, attribute, event, and directive completion (plus auto-import on accept) are provided by the nmbl VS Code extension. Make sure it's installed and reload the window. Note: a component's **own props/emits** aren't suggested yet — type them and rely on type-checking to validate.

            ### CSS classes not linking to styles

            This only works with `<style scoped>` blocks. This is a Vue/Volar limitation that applies to all template languages, not just nmbl.

            ### Syntax errors with indentation

            Ensure your template starts at column 0 with no leading whitespace. nmbl uses indentation for structure, so the root element must not be indented.

            ### Template not compiling

            Check that you have `@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin` installed and listed **before** `@vitejs/plugin-vue` in your Vite config.

